// Canasta — formalized DSL (stress test, not part of the settled corpus).
//
// Source: https://www.pagat.com/rummy/canasta.html ("Basic Rules of Canasta").
// Four players in two fixed partnerships (seats 0+2 vs 1+3). Each turn a player
// draws (2 from stock, or the whole discard pile if they can immediately meld
// its top card) then may lay down any number of melds of 3+ same-rank cards
// (wild cards: 2s substitute, capped at 3 per meld and never outnumbering the
// meld's natural cards), then discards one card. A meld of 7+ cards is a
// canasta (natural = no wild cards, worth more). Red 3s are an automatic
// bonus/penalty, drawn face-up and replaced. Going out requires the
// partnership to already hold a canasta.
//
// ============================================================================
// SIMPLIFICATIONS (every deliberate cut from the Pagat basic rules, recorded
// per the stress-test brief — nothing below is silently dropped):
// ============================================================================
//
// 1. GAP (blocker): `cards:` cannot express "2 standard 52-card packs + 4
//    jokers = 108 cards." The grammar's `cards:` production accepts only a
//    bare stdlib deck NAME (cardlang/grammar/cardlang.lark: `cards: "cards"
//    ":" NAME`), resolved against a closed, hardcoded Python registry
//    (cardlang/runtime/values.py `DECKS`, cardlang/stdlib/values.py
//    `_SUITS_BY_DECK`/`_DECK_SIZE`) — there is no in-language deck-composition
//    syntax (the `cards: { suits: {...} }` block and `cards: standard52 + {
//    specials: [...] }` composition described in docs/decisions.md "Deck
//    declaration" do not exist in the grammar; every stdlib deck, including
//    every extended one like `tichu56`/`tarot78`/`coup15`, is pre-registered
//    in Python, not assembled from a game file). A game file cannot add a
//    deck, add jokers, or double an existing deck. Workaround: `cards:
//    standard52` (single 52-card pack, no jokers). This is the single most
//    consequential cut in this file — everything below cascades from it:
//      - No jokers at all (there is no "joker" card in any registered deck).
//        Only the four 2s are wild. The joker's 50-point value and its extra
//        wild-card slots are simply gone.
//      - A single 52-card pack means at most 4 copies of any rank exist
//        game-wide, so a 7-card canasta needs one partnership to hold every
//        copy of a rank plus wild 2s — vanishingly unlikely under random
//        play (and rare even under skilled play). Hands are dealt at 7
//        cards (not 11) and the game runs a fixed 4 hands (not to 5000
//        cumulative) — see cuts 6 and 10 below — specifically so the game
//        terminates promptly despite this.
//
// 2. Fixed minimum initial-meld value (50), not the cumulative-score-scaled
//    table (negative score: 15; 0-1495: 50; 1500-2995: 90; 3000+: 120). A
//    partnership's very first meld of the hand must alone total >= 50 card
//    points (natural cards only — see cut 4); after that the partnership's
//    threshold is satisfied for the rest of the hand. (Suggested explicitly
//    by the stress-test brief as an acceptable cut.)
//
// 3. Black 3s (clubs/spades) can never be melded and can never be the top
//    card of a takeable discard pile (no `take_pile_as_three`/`meld_three`
//    move type exists) — this matches the real restriction, though for a
//    different proximate reason: only 2 black 3s exist in a single 52-card
//    pack, so a natural 3-of-a-kind is mathematically impossible anyway (see
//    cut 1). Black 3s are ordinary discardable dead cards. The real "black 3
//    may be melded only as your final going-out action" rule (a pair, or
//    with all remaining cards) is dropped along with it, since it's now moot.
//
// 4. GAP (major): wild cards cannot be added to a specific meld as a distinct
//    player decision, and a new meld's initial-value check only counts
//    natural cards. Melding is expressed as one move_type per rank
//    (`meld_<rank>`, `when:`-guarded, effect routes into that rank's own
//    static `TeamPile` zone — see cut 5 for why) whose effect moves *every*
//    matching-rank natural card from hand AND automatically tops up with the
//    maximum legal number of wild 2s from hand in the same action (capped at
//    3 total wild and at wild <= natural, per real rules). A player cannot
//    hold 2s back in hand while melding that rank, nor choose to add wilds
//    without also being able to add naturals. Re-triggering the same
//    `meld_<rank>` move later (naturals = 0, meld already exists) is legal
//    and _is_ how a later-drawn 2 gets added on its own — so the mechanic is
//    exercised, just without the fine per-wild-card choice real Canasta
//    gives. `min`/`max` are not callable stdlib functions (see gap list at
//    the end), so the wild-count cap is computed with a small local `min2`
//    function instead.
//
// 5. GAP (major): a team's melds cannot be one 2-D zone family indexed by
//    (team, rank) — `zone_decl` supports exactly one bracketed index
//    (cardlang/grammar/cardlang.lark: `zone_decl: NAME [index] ":"
//    type_ref`). Each meldable rank gets its own zone name
//    (`meld_two[team]`, `meld_four[team]`, ... `meld_ace[team]`, 12 zones),
//    each a plain `TeamPile<team>`. Combined with cut 4's "no dynamic
//    zone-by-value lookup" (a move effect must name a static zone; there is
//    no `meld_zone_for(rank)` indirection), melding unavoidably becomes one
//    move_type per rank rather than a single parameterized `meld(rank:
//    Rank)` move — see the gap list for why the parameterized form doesn't
//    work either (only `Suit`/`Suit?` move-parameter domains are
//    runtime-enumerable; `cardlang/runtime/mechanics.py::enumerate_domain`
//    has no `Rank` case, and `round offering [...]` is the only construct
//    that enumerates a parameterized move type over its domain — plain
//    `offer` doesn't enumerate move-type parameters at all).
//
// 6. Hands are dealt 7 cards, not 11 (52 - 4*7 = 24 stock cards instead of
//    52 - 4*11 = 8) — needed purely so a hand has enough stock left to
//    exercise more than a couple of draws, given cut 1 already halves the
//    deck. The hand ends the instant fewer than 2 stock cards remain (an
//    exact "stock is empty" check would let `draw_from_stock`'s own guard
//    try to deal 2 cards from a 1-card stock and crash) — a few unusable
//    stock cards are simply abandoned at that point, standing in for "the
//    stock depletes," one of Pagat's three listed hand-ending conditions.
//
// 7. Frozen-pile take is simplified to a single generic "take the whole
//    pile into hand, then meld every natural card of the top card's rank"
//    move per rank (`take_pile_as_<rank>`), rather than modelling "meld only
//    the top card, keep the rest of the pile in hand to use freely." Since
//    every card of a rank is interchangeable here (Canasta has no suit-based
//    legality at all), taking the whole pile and auto-routing same-rank
//    cards into the meld is behaviourally equivalent to "meld the top card"
//    for scoring purposes; it just also auto-melds any *other* cards of that
//    same rank that happened to be buried lower in the pile, which the
//    authentic rule would leave the player free to hold back. The frozen/
//    not-frozen legality distinction itself (2 natural cards always
//    sufficient; 1 natural card sufficient only if not frozen AND the
//    partnership already has a meld of that rank) IS implemented faithfully
//    — see `frozen_for`.
//
// 8. GAP (major): there is no `.top` / `expose_top` access to "the top card
//    of a zone" (docs/library.md and docs/decisions.md describe
//    `expose_top(zone)` as sugar for `reveal(zone.top, all)`, but the
//    grammar's `epistemic_op` production implements only `shuffle`
//    (cardlang/grammar/cardlang.lark: `epistemic_op: "shuffle" zone_expr ->
//    shuffle_op`, no other alternative); `reveal`/`peek`/`hide`/
//    `announce`/`expose_top`/`forget` are documented but not parseable, and
//    `Zone` exposes no `.top` member in cardlang/runtime/evaluate.py's
//    `_member`). So "what rank is on top of the discard pile" is tracked by
//    hand with an explicit `last_discard_rank : String` state variable, set
//    to a literal rank string by each per-rank `discard_<rank>` move type's
//    own effect (which already knows its rank statically) — this is also
//    why discards are one move type per rank rather than a single generic
//    `move chosen one card from hand[actor] to discard_pile` (which would
//    leave no way to learn what was actually discarded). The discard pile
//    itself is not seeded from stock at the start of a hand (real rules turn
//    the top stock card up); it simply starts empty, which is
//    observationally identical except that nobody can ever legally take it
//    on the very first turn — true anyway, since an empty pile holds nothing
//    to meld.
//
// 9. The "must have a canasta to go out" rule is enforced at scoring, not as
//    move legality. GAP (major): gating a `discard_<rank>` move on "if this
//    is your last card, your team must already have a canasta" is only safe
//    when at least one *other* legal move exists at that decision — but when
//    a hand holds exactly one card, exactly one `discard_<rank>` move type
//    is even a candidate (the one matching that card's rank), so blocking it
//    would leave the decision with zero legal moves, an engine error (see
//    docs/decisions.md "No implicit actions": a decision must always have a
//    legal move; there is no silent skip). So a discard that empties a hand
//    is always legal; instead, the scoring phase checks whether the
//    partnership that went out already held a canasta at that moment, and
//    withholds the 100-point going-out bonus if not (`team_has_canasta`).
//    This preserves the rule's scoring consequence without the crash risk of
//    a look-ahead-free legality gate.
//
// 10. Fixed 4-hand game (`hands_played >= 4`), not "first partnership to
//     5000 cumulative points." Cuts 1 and 6 make most hands score only a
//     handful of points (few melds ever complete), so "first to 5000" could
//     run for an impractical number of hands in a random playout. Mirrors
//     French Tarot's own `hands_played >= 36` fixed-length pattern
//     (docs/games/french-tarot.cardlang).
//
// 11. Going-out concealed (+200) and the score-crossing edge cases around
//     red 3s (e.g. a partnership holding all four) are dropped; a plain
//     +100 going-out bonus and a flat +/-100 per red 3 are kept.
//
// See the structured stress-test report for the full language-gap catalogue
// (closed deck registry; closed stdlib-function dispatch table so no new
// game-local runtime primitive can be added without writing Python, which is
// out of scope here; single-index zone families; no dynamic zone-by-value
// lookup; no `.top`/`expose_top`; no callable `min`/`max`; the `count`
// aggregator returns the *collection size*, not a filtered count — it
// ignores its body entirely, so every filtered count in this file uses `sum
// over ... as c: if <pred> then 1 else 0` instead, matching existing corpus
// idiom (docs/games/pinochle.cardlang)).

game Canasta {

  players: 4
  partnerships: [[0, 2], [1, 3]]
  direction: clockwise

  cards: standard52

  zones {
    stock              : Deck
    hand[player]        : Hand<player>
    discard_pile         : Discard
    meld_two[team]       : TeamPile<team>
    meld_four[team]      : TeamPile<team>
    meld_five[team]      : TeamPile<team>
    meld_six[team]       : TeamPile<team>
    meld_seven[team]     : TeamPile<team>
    meld_eight[team]     : TeamPile<team>
    meld_nine[team]      : TeamPile<team>
    meld_ten[team]       : TeamPile<team>
    meld_jack[team]      : TeamPile<team>
    meld_queen[team]     : TeamPile<team>
    meld_king[team]      : TeamPile<team>
    meld_ace[team]       : TeamPile<team>
    red_threes[team]     : TeamPile<team>
  }

  state {
    // Game-level: persist across hands.
    score[team]  : Integer = 0
    hands_played : Integer = 0
  }

  phase hand_sequence repeats until hands_played >= 4 {
    state {
      dealer            : Player  = 0
      has_melded[team]  : Boolean = false
      last_discard_rank : String  = ""
      pile_has_wild     : Boolean = false
    }

    before_each {
      move all cards to stock
      shuffle stock
      deal 7 cards from stock to each hand
      dealer := dealer offset_by left
      for each team t: has_melded[t] := false
      last_discard_rank := ""
      pile_has_wild := false

      // Red 3s: immediately face up, replaced from stock (cascades if a
      // replacement is itself a red 3).
      for each player p: repeat until (sum over hand[p] as c: if is_red_three(c) then 1 else 0) == 0 {
        let r3 = sum over hand[p] as c: if is_red_three(c) then 1 else 0
        move all cards from hand[p] where c => is_red_three(c) to red_threes[team_of(p)]
        if r3 <= (count over stock as c: true) {
          move r3 cards from stock to hand[p]
        } else {
          move all cards from stock to hand[p]
        }
      }
    }

    phase play {
      state {
        active       : Player  = 0
        melding_done : Boolean = false
      }

      active := dealer offset_by left

      repeat until (count over stock as c: true) < 2 or (any player p: hand[p] is empty) {
        offer to active one of [
          draw_from_stock,
          take_pile_as_two, take_pile_as_four, take_pile_as_five, take_pile_as_six,
          take_pile_as_seven, take_pile_as_eight, take_pile_as_nine, take_pile_as_ten,
          take_pile_as_jack, take_pile_as_queen, take_pile_as_king, take_pile_as_ace
        ]

        melding_done := false
        repeat until melding_done {
          offer to active one of [
            meld_two, meld_four, meld_five, meld_six, meld_seven, meld_eight,
            meld_nine, meld_ten, meld_jack, meld_queen, meld_king, meld_ace,
            stop_melding
          ]
        }

        if hand[active] is not empty {
          offer to active one of [
            discard_two, discard_three, discard_four, discard_five, discard_six,
            discard_seven, discard_eight, discard_nine, discard_ten, discard_jack,
            discard_queen, discard_king, discard_ace
          ]
        }

        active := active offset_by left
      }
    }

    phase scoring {
      for each team t: score[t] += meld_points_of(t)
      for each player p: score[team_of(p)] -= hand_penalty_of(p)
      for each team t: score[t] += red_three_bonus(t)
      for each team t: score[t] += canasta_bonus_of(t)

      // Going-out bonus: withheld unless the going-out partnership already
      // held a canasta (cut 9 — enforced here, not as move legality).
      let went_out = number of players where hand[player] is empty
      if went_out == 1 {
        let outp = the player where hand[player] is empty
        if team_has_canasta(team_of(outp)) {
          score[team_of(outp)] += 100
        }
      }
    }

    after_each {
      hands_played += 1
    }
  }

  winner: highest score
}

// =============================================================================
// === Move vocabulary ===
// =============================================================================

// --- Draw or take (one per turn) ---

move_type draw_from_stock {
  when: (count over stock as c: true) >= 2
  effect {
    move 2 cards from stock to hand[actor]
    repeat until (sum over hand[actor] as c: if is_red_three(c) then 1 else 0) == 0 {
      let r3 = sum over hand[actor] as c: if is_red_three(c) then 1 else 0
      move all cards from hand[actor] where c => is_red_three(c) to red_threes[team_of(actor)]
      if r3 <= (count over stock as c: true) {
        move r3 cards from stock to hand[actor]
      } else {
        move all cards from stock to hand[actor]
      }
    }
  }
}

move_type take_pile_as_two {
  when: discard_pile is not empty and last_discard_rank == "2" and
        (has_melded[team_of(actor)] or (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "2" then point_value(c) else 0) >= 50) and
        (naturals_of(actor, "2") >= 2 or
         (naturals_of(actor, "2") >= 1 and meld_two[team_of(actor)] is not empty and not frozen_for(team_of(actor))))
  effect {
    move all cards from discard_pile to hand[actor]
    pile_has_wild := false
    move all cards from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "2" to meld_two[team_of(actor)]
    has_melded[team_of(actor)] := true
  }
}

move_type take_pile_as_four {
  when: discard_pile is not empty and last_discard_rank == "4" and
        (has_melded[team_of(actor)] or (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "4" then point_value(c) else 0) >= 50) and
        (naturals_of(actor, "4") >= 2 or
         (naturals_of(actor, "4") >= 1 and meld_four[team_of(actor)] is not empty and not frozen_for(team_of(actor))))
  effect {
    move all cards from discard_pile to hand[actor]
    pile_has_wild := false
    move all cards from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "4" to meld_four[team_of(actor)]
    has_melded[team_of(actor)] := true
  }
}

move_type take_pile_as_five {
  when: discard_pile is not empty and last_discard_rank == "5" and
        (has_melded[team_of(actor)] or (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "5" then point_value(c) else 0) >= 50) and
        (naturals_of(actor, "5") >= 2 or
         (naturals_of(actor, "5") >= 1 and meld_five[team_of(actor)] is not empty and not frozen_for(team_of(actor))))
  effect {
    move all cards from discard_pile to hand[actor]
    pile_has_wild := false
